---
title: "Layout: escaping the text column"
---

The reading measure is deliberately narrow, around 70 characters, because that is what
long-form prose wants. Some content does not: a wide table, a multi-panel plot, a
diagram whose labels only fit at size. `.column-margin` already sends a note *out* to
the gutter; these two send a block *wider* instead, and they are the same axis.

::: {.column-margin}
This margin note is the narrow end of the same axis the two escapes below widen along.
It shares a page with them on purpose: a document usually wants both.
:::

## `.column-page`, past the text column {#sec-page}

`::: {.column-page}` widens a block up to the page width. The prose around it stays at
the reading measure, so the escape reads as a deliberate exception rather than as a
change of layout.

::: {.column-page}
| Container | How an escape grows | Sticky rail in the way |
| --- | --- | --- |
| single-document `body` | centred on the viewport | no |
| `body.has-toc` | leftward, right edge flush with the text | yes |
| `.tali-site-main` | centred on the viewport | no |
| `.tali-site-main.has-toc` | leftward, right edge flush with the text | yes |
| `.tali-book-main` | centred on the viewport | no |
:::

That table is the reason the feature is one CSS formula rather than five. An escape
centres on *its own container*, and the three plain containers are themselves centred in
the viewport, so nothing in them needs to know which one it is.

The two table-of-contents layouts are the exception, and the reason is visible on this
page: the rail to the right is ordinary text with no background of its own, so an escape
centred on the page would run its own text underneath it. On those pages an escape grows
*leftward* into the unused margin and keeps its right edge flush with the prose, which is
collision-free by construction and still recovers most of the wasted width.

## `.column-screen`, edge to edge {#sec-screen}

`::: {.column-screen}` goes the whole width of the viewport, with no gutter left at
all. It is for content that is its own full-bleed band.

::: {.column-screen}
Edge to edge. On a phone this is the whole screen. On a page with a table-of-contents
rail it stops at the prose column's right edge instead, for the same reason
`.column-page` does, so "screen" there means every pixel that is not already spoken for.
:::

## They are plain classes {#sec-plain}

Neither is a new block type, so neither needs a `:::` container of its own when the
thing being widened is already a block. They apply to a table, a figure, a `pre`, or a
div alike, and the block keeps its own identity, which is what lets a cross-reference
still resolve through one.

::: {.column-page}
```python
# A listing wide enough to want the room, inside an escape.
records = [(name, kind, centre, offset) for name, kind, centre, offset in containers]
```
:::

Back at the reading measure, and the paragraph after an escape sits exactly where the
paragraph before it did.
